{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210736,"nonce":"OdCQjzkx+IK3Crih7ytD+CQXSec=","title":"AOT package loading: full hatch spec corpus under AOT","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n**Spec audit progression** (per-package `*.spec.wren` under `wlift … --aot`):\n\n| Stage | RUN-PASS | RUN-TEST-FAIL | RUN-NO-SUMMARY | BUILD-FAIL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pre-fix | 1 | 0 | 38 | 3 (gpu/assets/window) |\n| Phase 0 (loader: transitive scoped resolver + `cfg::apply`) | 24-ish | 0 | 16 | 0 |\n| Phase 1 (Fiber.try abort propagation) | 11 | 13 | 16 | 0 |\n| Phase 2 (AOT-stub fast path sets `ctx.closure`) | 14 | 18 | 9 | 0 |\n| Phase 3 (diamond-import aliases) | 15 | 18 | 8 | 0 |\n| Phase 4 (SM dispatch ctx.closure + jit_roots_snapshot var) | 15 | 19 | 7 | 0 |\n| Phase 4b (vm.rs: error not panic on empty MIR) | 15 | 20 | 6 | 0 |\n| Phase 5a (rebuild stale plugin dylibs) | 17 | 20 | 4 | 0 |\n| Phase 5b (`wren_aot_check_error` poll at AOT block entry) | 23 | 15 | 3 | 0 |\n| Phase 5c (unbounded closure upvalues + SM closure dispatch + workspace fallback) | 24 | 17 | 0 | 0 |\n| Phase 6a (`call_jit_cached` arity 8) | 31 | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n| Phase 6b (call_closure_sync runs without a current fiber + arity match) | 32 | 9 | 0 | 0 |\n| Phase 6c (bare-name imports + re-export binding lookup) | 34 | 6 | 0 | 0 |\n| Phase 6d (dup-method CHA + wide-arity wren_call_dynamic) | 36 | 5 | 0 | 0 |\n| Phase 6e (has_error short-circuit in dispatch_call_rooted) | **39** | **2** | **0** | **0** |\n\n**Phase 0 — package loader (commit c72ebd9):**\n- `build_scoped_resolver` BFS-walks transitive `path` deps (visited-deduped) so a spec running from `hatch-fp/` reaches `@hatch:fmt` via `@hatch:test`'s own hatchfile. Pre-fix: only direct deps wired up; transitive deps left unresolved → AOT body's symbols read null at runtime.\n- `build_aot_module_from_source` and `walk_module` run source through `crate::parse::cfg::apply(_, None)` before parsing. Same upstream filter `hatch build` and `wlift run` already apply. Pre-fix: packages with `#!native` / `#!wasm` parallel imports tripped resolver's \"module variable already defined\".\n\n**Phase 1 — Fiber.try error propagation (commit d4ee31c):**\n- `vm_interp::run_fiber` AOT-stub fast path checks `vm.has_error` after the body returns. Under `is_try`, routes the runtime error through `route_method_error_through_fiber_try` (the same hook every BC-interp method-dispatch boundary uses) and clears `vm.has_error` so the runtime-side report fires once, not twice.\n- `handle_jit_fiber_action::Call`'s \"no caller\" fall-through (top-level AOT body running directly from `wlift_aot_main`, before any user-created fiber sets `vm.fiber`) surfaces `target.error` when the target finished `Done` under `is_try`. Without this, `route` writes were correct but the dispatcher returned `Ok(default)`, binding `null` at the AOT call site of `try()`.\n\n**Phase 2 — JIT-context closure pointer (commit bd4c95b):**\n- `call_closure_jit_or_sync` (the `wren_call_*` path) sets `ctx.closure = closure_ptr` before invoking the JIT/AOT body so its `wren_load_jit_closure` (every GetUpvalue / SetUpvalue site) reads the right pointer. The AOT-stub fast path in `vm_interp::run_fiber` bypasses `wren_call_*` entirely and was missing the same setup. Symptom: any `Fiber.new { ... }` whose body captured a function-local upvalue dereferenced null on first upvalue read, SIGSEGV. The hatch test framework's `Test.it(\"name\") { var x = ...; var e = Fiber.new { x.foo() }.try() }` pattern surfaced it in 9 / 41 specs.\n\n**Phase 3 — diamond-import aliases (commit 89ddea0):**\n- The walker dedupes a re-visited module on its canonical path. The import-binding pass matched manifests by `module_name` (the FIRST `request_name` the walker recorded, e.g. `./fmt`). A transitive importer using a different spelling (`@hatch:fmt`) found no manifest, fell through to `runtime_imports`, and the symbol read null at use time.\n- Fix: walker records every alternate `request_name` on `AotModule.aliases` (and its manifest's `module_aliases`). Binding pass matches `module_name` OR any alias. `hatch-fmt`'s self-spec (the inception case — `import \"./fmt\"` + `import \"@hatch:test\"` which transitively imports `@hatch:fmt`) now passes 21 / 21.\n\n**Phase 4b — empty-MIR method dispatch (commit 032897b):**\n- `vm.rs` method-dispatch fall-through hit `mir.blocks[0]` with an empty MIR Vec when an AOT-stub function had neither a registered `jit_code` pointer nor a foreign binding. Replaced the panic with `has_error = true` + a runtime error so the fiber-level path can route it. Unblocks hatch-template (was killing the host process at `wren_call_7` mid-spec).\n\n**Phase 5a — rebuild plugin dylibs:**\n- The dylibs at `hatch/packages/hatch-{audio,gpu,image,physics,sqlite,window}/libs/*.dylib` were built April-May against an older VM struct layout; the plugin uses `(*vm).api_stack` directly via `wren_lift::runtime::vm::VM` (the documented \"must enable wren_lift/host\" pattern), so the inlined offsets stale-fault at the first foreign-method call. `cargo build --release -p wlift_\u003cname\u003e` against the current host then `cp target/release/libwlift_\u003cname\u003e.dylib hatch/packages/hatch-\u003cname\u003e/libs/` recovered every native package. (Not committed: `libs/` is `.gitignore`d.)\n\n**Phase 5b — has_error poll at AOT block entry (commit 863ca34):**\n- The BC interp re-checks `vm.has_error` after every opcode dispatch and unwinds the fiber on an in-flight error; AOT-compiled Cranelift bodies have no boundary. A `Fiber.abort` in the body of a `while (true) { … }` loop fires once but Cranelift's straight-line code keeps iterating, re-aborting at successive offsets. Symptom: `JSON.parse(\"{\")` produced 84 MB of repeat-abort output before timing out.\n- Fix: new runtime helper `wren_aot_check_error` (returns 1 if `vm.has_error`); the Cranelift backend lazy-creates a per-function `current_abort_exit_block`, polls `wren_aot_check_error` at the top of every non-entry block, and `brif`-branches to that block when set. The exit block restores `current_jit_roots_snapshot_var` and returns a typed null so the AOT-stub fast path's existing has_error route in `vm_interp::run_fiber` surfaces the error normally.\n- Specs that flipped to PASS through this fix alone: hatch-{json, csv, events, path, time, url, uuid}. All used `Fiber.abort` inside `Test.it` bodies under `Fiber.try`, where the framework caught the abort once but the AOT body kept running side-effects after the catch.\n\n**Phase 5c — closure upvalues / SM dispatch / workspace fallback (commit acf47b0):**\n- `MakeClosure` lowering truncated past 4 captures; `Session.cookie`'s 7-upvalue middleware read past `ObjClosure.upvalues` end and SIGSEGVd. Added `wren_make_closure_5..8` plus a generic `wren_make_closure_n(fn_id, count, ptr)` slow-path with stack-spilled args.\n- SM-tainted closures invoked via `Fn.call(_)` crashed at `wlift_aot_sm_load_state` because regular dispatch passed `(receiver, ...args)` into a `(fiber, resume_v)` body. `call_closure_jit_or_sync` now detects SM closures, pushes a child `aot_frames` entry, and routes through the poll fn with a Yield → `pending_fiber_action` translation.\n- `build_scoped_resolver` only walked path-linked deps; a monorepo package that pinned `@hatch:io = \"0.2.0\"` (rather than path-linking) had its `import \"@hatch:io\"` left unresolved. Map `@hatch:\u003cname\u003e` → `\u003cworkspace_parent\u003e/hatch-\u003cname\u003e` when the sibling has a hatchfile.\n\n**Phase 6a — `call_jit_cached` arity 8 (commit 2a1e8a0):**\n- `call_jit_cached` and `call_jit_with_shadow_raw` transmuted every \u003e3-arg JIT body to a 4-arg fn pointer and dropped trailing args. A 7-arg `Render_.renderFullWithFrags` body invoked with 4 args read register garbage; 81 of 96 template specs failed with the same \"method dispatch hit AOT stub with no body\" cascade. Added 5/6/7/8-arg arms. Lifted the closure-dispatch arity gate from `\u003c= 4` to `\u003c= 8` in both `dispatch_method`'s Closure branch and `call_closure_jit_or_sync`. Mirrored Phase 4b's panic-to-error guard in `vm.call_constructor_sync`.\n\n**Phase 6b — call_closure_sync runs without a current fiber + arity match (commit f131cb3):**\n- AOT-stub fast path was gated on `!self.fiber.is_null()`; native-side helpers (`call_method_on`'s `starts_with(\"call\")` shortcut, the sequence helpers in `core/sequence.rs`) reach call_closure_sync before any user fiber has been pushed. The fast path's `call_jit_fn_pub` only depends on TLS `JitContext`, so apply it before the fiber gate.\n- The fast path unconditionally prepended the closure pointer at slot 0 — correct for `Method::Closure` callers (`args = [recv, user...]`, body arity = 1 + user_count) but wrong for `Fn.call(_)` shortcuts (`args = [user...]`, body arity = user_count). Now branches on `args.len() \u003c body_arity`: prepend only when the body has an implicit receiver the caller didn't supply. Fixes the prelude `MapSequence._fn.call(value)` shape, which was reading the closure object as `n` and returning null.\n\n**Phase 6c — bare-name imports + re-export bindings (commit 2b0fbba):**\n- The walker only resolved `./X`, `../X`, and `@hatch:X`. Bare names (`import \"assets_native\" for ...`) were silently dropped, used by every cross-target re-export module. Fall back to a sibling file when the importer's directory has `\u003cname\u003e.wren`.\n- The cross-module import-binding pass looked only at the source manifest's `classes` list; for re-export modules (no class definitions, just imports) the lookup found nothing and the binding silently dropped. Walk through the source's `module_var_names` as a fallback so the slot index lands on whatever the re-exporter populated by its OWN earlier import binding. Chains `import \"./re_export\" for X` → `import \"src\" for X` correctly across both hops.\n\n**Phase 6d — dup-method CHA + wide-arity dispatch (commit 0917da6):**\n- A class declaring the same `name(_)` signature twice has the second body win at runtime (install loop overwrites `cls.methods[sym]` in declaration order). The CHA dispatch tree appended every impl, so the class-checked direct call inlined the FIRST body at every call site. The regex spec's two-arity `group(_)` (one for List index, one for named-groups Map) was running the wrong arm. `build_cha` now replaces any existing impl on the same class instead of appending.\n- AOT lowering aborted the build with \"Call with arity N not supported by JIT (max 8)\" for any \u003e 8-arg Call. `@hatch:gpu`'s 12-arg pipeline-setup methods made the entire package un-buildable. Added `wren_call_dynamic(receiver, method, count, ptr)` runtime helper; codegen spills args into a stack-allocated `[u64; n]` for the wide path. CHA / IC fast paths stay scoped to ≤ 8.\n\n**Phase 6e — has_error short-circuit in dispatch_call (commit f389b8f):**\n- The block-entry `wren_aot_check_error` poll catches an in-flight error at the next branch boundary, but a single basic block can hold multiple Call sites. After `Fiber.abort` fires, every subsequent Call in the same block still ran, and whichever one validated args first overwrote the original error message. The sqlite spec's `db.close; db.execute(...)` case made this concrete: `checkAlive_()` aborted with \"Database: use after close\", but `SqliteCore.execute(_id=null, ...)` then fired its own \"id must be a non-negative integer\" and that's what `Fiber.try` returned.\n- Fix: the first thing `dispatch_call_rooted` (and its no-frame fast path) does after fetching the VM is `if vm.has_error { return Value::null() }`. Mirrors the BC interpreter's per-opcode short-circuit. The block-entry poll then catches the original error at the next branch.\n- **Important debugging takeaway:** I'd initially classified the sqlite / http / crypto failures as \"test-data drift\". The user pushed back asking whether I'd verified against JIT / interpreter — and they were right: these were AOT-only bugs (the in-CI JIT path catches them via per-opcode `has_error`). Don't dismiss anything as \"test data wrong\" without comparing the same spec across modes.\n\n**Phase 6f — defensive cross-fn invoker fallback + bench-build cfg gate (commits d8e2114, 6dbd44d):**\n- `bench/run.sh` runs `cargo build --release` (no `--features aot`); the build was failing silently because `cranelift_backend::AotLoweringConfig` referenced `aot_state_machine::StateMachineLayout` / `BlockKind` directly without an `aot` cfg gate. Three E0432/E0433 errors got swallowed by the script's `2\u003e/dev/null` and `set -e` aborted before any benchmark ran. Fixed by gating the field declaration and every read site with `#[cfg(feature = \"aot\")]`.\n- `wlift_aot_invoke_sm_method` now falls back to `call_closure_jit_or_sync` when the resolved closure isn't SM-tainted (instead of returning null). Reads args back from the topmost aot_frame, dispatches normally, pops the frame, stamps `Done`. Picks Fn.call vs method calling convention via `closure_ptr_from_recv.is_some()`. Top-level bodies are forced non-SM regardless of taint (entry-point ABI fixed at `() -\u003e i64`). These are defensive — the call-site that would route non-SM closures here doesn't exist yet.\n\n**Remaining (2 RUN-TEST-FAIL specs, 4 fails):**\n- Genuine cross-fn fiber-yield bugs (io's `Reader.withTryFn`, proc's `stdoutAsync`): a yielding helper closure invoked via `Fn.call` from a non-tainted outer body. The outer body keeps running past the yield because it isn't SM-transformed.\n- Tried adding `call(N)` to the taint set whenever any closure is SM (so outer chains become SM-aware via the v2-cap-3 cross-fn lowering). The cross-fn invoker fallback above lets that not break non-SM closures, BUT the broader SM coverage exposes a pre-existing dominance violation in the SM transform's Cranelift output: for `@hatch:proc`'s closure_50 the verifier rejects with `\"uses value v494 from non-dominating inst555\"`. Some MIR pattern's live-across-yield values aren't being captured by `yield_saves` / `resume_loads`. Held until that's fixed in `aot_state_machine`.\n\n**Path to closing the last 2:**\n1. In `aot_state_machine.rs`, run a proper per-block liveness pass over the post-transform CFG. Currently the transform pre-computes `yield_saves[bid]` / `resume_loads[bid]` from a pattern-matching pass that misses values defined before a yield but used after a sibling yield's resume entry.\n2. Once that's solid, re-enable the `call(N)` taint expansion (the code is in commit history of the reverted Phase 6 attempt) — io's 2 fails and proc's 2 fails should both flip to PASS in one go.\n3. The existing cross-fn invoker fallback (commit 6dbd44d) becomes the load-bearing path for every non-yielding closure that gets dragged into SM dispatch through the broader taint.\n\n**18 RUN-TEST-FAIL specs:** test framework runs to completion, but assertions surface mismatches that are mostly:\n- Test-data drift (e.g. spec asserts `toContain \"empty\"` when actual abort message is `\"empty input\"`).\n- Missing native `.dylib` artefacts (image / window / gpu).\n- One or two genuine AOT correctness bugs in specific opcodes (math precision, buffer ops returning null) that warrant their own commit.\n\n**How to apply:** When debugging an AOT spec failure, the order to check is:\n1. Build fails / \"module variable already defined\" → Phase 0 fix should already cover.\n2. Spec runs but prints `null` lines or no test results → Phase 0 (transitive resolver), Phase 3 (diamond aliases).\n3. Spec aborts with SIGSEGV touching upvalues → Phase 2.\n4. Spec's `fiber.try` returns null → Phase 1.\n5. Spec runs forever / repeats the same abort message at growing offsets → Phase 5b (block-entry has_error poll).\n6. Spec calls into a `foreign class` and crashes at the first foreign call → Phase 5a (stale plugin dylib; rebuild via `cargo build -p wlift_\u003cname\u003e` and copy the `.dylib` into `hatch/packages/hatch-\u003cname\u003e/libs/`).\n7. Otherwise it's a runtime correctness bug specific to whatever's about to run.","files":null}]}